@@ -0,0 +1,218 @@
+# ForkMesh Repository, Node, and Replica Model v1
+
+This path is retained for existing links, but the document describes the Phase 0 version 1 contract rather than a discussion draft.
+The authoritative machine-readable files are [`repository.schema.json`](../../contracts/node/v1/repository.schema.json), [`node.schema.json`](../../contracts/node/v1/node.schema.json), [`replica.schema.json`](../../contracts/node/v1/replica.schema.json), [`capability.schema.json`](../../contracts/node/v1/capability.schema.json), [`ref-update.schema.json`](../../contracts/node/v1/ref-update.schema.json), and [`fixtures.json`](../../contracts/node/v1/fixtures.json) under [`contracts/node/v1`](../../contracts/node/v1/).
+
+## Phase 0 boundary
+
+Phase 0 implements closed and bounded schemas, deterministic fixtures, catalog projection and capability-routing examples, signed-ref payload and CAS examples, a corrected capability catalog and matrix, and focused contract tests.
+It does not implement production mobile key storage, embedded Git, repository persistence, Worker registration or routing endpoints, agent runtimes, ref-update signature processing, or durable cross-node commands.
+Those production capabilities remain planned for later phases.
+
+## Canonical opaque identifiers
+
+Repository, node, and replica IDs use a random opaque 128-bit value encoded as canonical unpadded base64url.
+The suffix is exactly 22 URL-safe characters that decode to 16 bytes, with no `=` padding or alternate encoding.
+Consumers must decode and canonically re-encode IDs so noncanonical spellings fail validation.
+
+| Identity | Exact form | Total length |
+|---|---|---:|
+| Repository | `fmrepo_` plus a canonical 16-byte base64url suffix | 29 |
+| Node | `fmnode_` plus a canonical 16-byte base64url suffix | 29 |
+| Replica | `fmreplica_` plus a canonical 16-byte base64url suffix | 32 |
+
+These IDs are not content hashes, filesystem paths, display names, account slugs, device labels, or capability claims.
+A registered node public key is separately encoded as canonical unpadded base64url for exactly 32 bytes, and a ref-update signature is encoded the same way for exactly 64 bytes.
+
+## Repository identity manifest
+
+The committed `.forkmesh/repository.json` object has exactly five fields:
+
+| Field | Contract fact |
+|---|---|
+| `version` | The integer `1`. |
+| `repoId` | The immutable canonical `fmrepo_` identifier. |
+| `createdBy` | The account that created the repository identity. |
+| `createdAt` | A bounded nonnegative integer timestamp in Unix epoch milliseconds. |
+| `defaultBranch` | The default branch short name, not a `refs/heads/` path. |
+
+Catalog owner, display name, public slug, clone locations, replicas, and mutable heads are intentionally outside the manifest.
+Renaming, transferring ownership, moving a clone, mirroring, and adding or removing hosts preserve the manifest and `repoId`.
+
+Cloning or mirroring copies the existing manifest and registers a new replica for the same `repoId`.
+Creating an independent repository generates a new `repoId`, even when another repository has the same display name or files.
+Repositories without a manifest require an explicit later migration rather than implicit identity guessing.
+If a presented manifest conflicts with an existing clone or replica binding, the operation fails closed and retains the prior binding.
+
+## Catalog projection
+
+Catalog projection records contain `ownerAccount`, `slug`, `displayName`, and `repoId`.
+The pair `(ownerAccount, slug)` is unique and cannot route to two different repository IDs.
+Display names may repeat.
+One `repoId` projects to one logical catalog repository, while its replicas and clone locations remain separate records.
+
+The Phase 0 fixture projects one `repoId` once with desktop, Android, and iOS replicas.
+It also contains an independently created repository with the same display name, a different `repoId`, and a different slug.
+
+## Registered node
+
+A node is one independently keyed registered installation.
+Desktop, Android, iOS, server, and headless are compatibility kinds, not authority levels.
+Flutter and Qt are implementations, and neither implementation receives implicit capability or trust.
+
+Every version 1 node record requires:
+
+| Field | Contract fact |
+|---|---|
+| `version` | The integer `1`. |
+| `nodeId` | The immutable canonical `fmnode_` identifier. |
+| `account` | The account to which registration binds the node. |
+| `deviceKind` | One of `desktop`, `android`, `ios`, `server`, or `headless`. |
+| `publicKey` | The node's canonical 32-byte public key encoding. |
+| `label` | A bounded user-facing label with no leading or trailing whitespace or control characters. |
+| `state` | Either `enabled` or `revoked`. |
+| `capabilities` | Between 1 and 128 atomic versioned advertisements, unique by capability ID. |
+| `lastSeenAt` | A bounded nonnegative integer presence timestamp in Unix epoch milliseconds. |
+
+Registration binds `nodeId`, account, and public key as distinct facts.
+The private key remains on the node, and planned production implementations must store it in platform-protected storage.
+`lastSeenAt` informs presence and routing but does not authorize an operation.
+A revoked node is ineligible even if an old advertisement or grant still exists.
+
+## Repository replica
+
+A replica binds exactly one logical `repoId` to exactly one registered `nodeId` under its own immutable `replicaId`.
+A mobile replica is intended to be persistent app-private repository state, not a disposable cache, once the later storage phases are implemented.
+
+Every version 1 replica record requires:
+
+| Field | Contract fact |
+|---|---|
+| `version` | The integer `1`. |
+| `replicaId` | The immutable canonical `fmreplica_` identifier. |
+| `repoId` | The logical repository identity. |
+| `nodeId` | The registered node that stores the replica. |
+| `completeness` | One of `full`, `partial`, or `metadata-only`. |
+| `availability` | One of `offline`, `active-only`, `background`, or `always`. |
+| `canonicalHead` | The last known canonical `{ref, sha}` pair, or null. |
+| `fetchedHead` | The locally fetched `{ref, sha}` pair, or null. |
+| `lastSyncedAt` | A bounded integer synchronization timestamp, or null. |
+| `lastServedAt` | A bounded integer serving timestamp, or null. |
+| `storageBytes` | The bounded nonnegative stored byte count. |
+| `servingCapabilities` | The atomic serving capability IDs the replica currently offers. |
+
+Each non-null head contains exactly a canonical `refs/heads/...` ref and a lowercase nonzero 40-character or 64-character hexadecimal object ID.
+Replica completeness, availability, timestamps, size, and serving modes must describe reality.
+The serving capability IDs must be present and enabled in the owning node's advertisements.
+A full replica may still be `offline` or `active-only`, especially on mobile platforms with background scheduling limits.
+
+## Atomic capabilities, roles, and grants
+
+One capability advertisement has exactly `id`, `version`, and `enabled`, plus an optional `constraints` object.
+The version is `1`, and capability IDs are atomic so independently risky operations can be granted and revoked separately.
+A node must not advertise the same capability ID more than once.
+
+Supported constraint facts are:
+
+- `platforms` for compatible device kinds.
+- `runtimes` for required execution runtimes.
+- `availability` ordered as `offline < active-only < background < always`, where the advertisement must meet or exceed the requested level.
+- `maxRepositoryBytes` for a storage or execution size ceiling.
+- `networkPolicy`, where `any` accepts `offline`, `online`, or `unmetered`, `online` accepts `online` or `unmetered`, and `unmetered` accepts only `unmetered`.
+- `chargingPolicy`, where `any` and `preferred` accept either charging state, `required` needs charging, and `preferred` influences selection preference only.
+- `supportedToolIds` for the structured tools an executor can expose.
+
+An advertisement is a capability claim, not permission.
+Independent `repositoryRoles` fixture records contain exactly `version`, `roleId`, `repoId`, `actorAccount`, and `repoRole`.
+There is at most one authoritative role mapping for each `(repoId, actorAccount)` pair.
+Explicit repository-scoped grants are separate fixture records with `version`, `grantId`, `repoId`, `nodeId`, `actorAccount`, `repoRole`, `enabled`, `expiresAt`, and `capabilityIds`.
+The grant's role must equal the independent authoritative role, so a grant cannot invent a repository role or elevate the actor.
+The grant narrows that role to its bound actor, repository, node, expiry, enabled state, and atomic capability IDs.
+
+After actor authentication, authorization requires every gate to pass:
+
+1. The node belongs to the trusted registration membership and is `enabled`.
+2. The node's registered `account` equals the authenticated actor account.
+3. Exactly one independent `repositoryRoles` record supplies the required role for the repository and actor.
+4. An explicit enabled and unexpired repository-scoped grant matches the repository, actor, node, and authoritative role and includes every requested capability.
+5. Every requested atomic capability is advertised uniquely and `enabled`.
+6. Every advertised constraint matches the request context.
+
+`deviceKind` is used only for compatibility matching.
+It never substitutes for a role, grant, advertisement, or constraint check.
+
+## Execution and agent capabilities
+
+`node-execution` is local-first on the requesting eligible node.
+When local eligibility fails, the user may optionally route a typed request to another trusted eligible node.
+Local mobile capability never requires pairing with Qt.
+
+Mobile nodes may later advertise API-backed agents such as `agent.openai_api` and `agent.anthropic_api` with structured capabilities such as `tool.files.read`, `tool.files.write`, `tool.git.status`, `tool.git.diff`, and `tool.validation.safe`.
+Mobile agents receive bounded repository tools rather than a general shell.
+Desktop or server nodes may optionally advertise CLI agents, shell, Docker, native workflows, and release signing when their grants and constraints permit them.
+These are capability choices, not privileges inherent in desktop or server device kinds.
+
+Provider secrets, signing keys, and other executor credentials remain local to the node performing the operation.
+The Worker coordinates authorization and routing but must not receive or synchronize raw provider secrets.
+The Phase 0 fixtures model eligibility only; production agent tools, secret stores, and cross-node executors are planned.
+
+## Signed canonical ref update
+
+A version 1 update envelope contains exactly these required fields:
+
+1. `version`
+2. `repoId`
+3. `ref`
+4. `expectedOldSha`
+5. `newSha`
+6. `actorAccount`
+7. `nodeId`
+8. `createdAt`
+9. `expiresAt`
+10. `idempotencyKey`
+11. `signature`
+
+`ref` is a canonical branch ref under `refs/heads/`.
+`newSha` is lowercase hexadecimal with exactly 40 characters for SHA-1 repositories or 64 characters for SHA-256 repositories, and an all-zero object ID is invalid.
+For an existing branch, `expectedOldSha` is a nonzero lowercase SHA with exactly the same width as `newSha` and must equal the current canonical head.
+For branch creation, `expectedOldSha` is null and is accepted only when the ref does not already exist.
+
+`createdAt`, `expiresAt`, and `version` must arrive as JSON integers without fraction or exponent spellings.
+The Phase 0 checks require `expiresAt` to be later than `createdAt`, but rejection based on the processing clock remains later production work.
+`idempotencyKey` is a lowercase UUID-form key that identifies one logical mutation.
+Phase 0 validates its shape, while durable reuse of a prior result for a duplicate key remains later production work.
+
+The contract defines the detached Ed25519 signature input as the UTF-8 bytes of the RFC 8785 JSON Canonicalization Scheme form of exactly these unsigned fields:
+
+```text
+actorAccount
+createdAt
+expectedOldSha
+expiresAt
+idempotencyKey
+newSha
+nodeId
+ref
+repoId
+version
+```
+
+The canonical object uses lexical key order, compact separators, no trailing newline, and excludes `signature`.
+Production signature verification will use the public key bound to the trusted registered enabled node.
+The focused Phase 0 ref-update path checks that `node.account` matches the authenticated actor, exactly one independent repository role exists and is `owner` or `maintainer`, a matching enabled and unexpired grant includes `ref.canonical.update`, and the node advertises that capability as enabled.
+It also checks that `expiresAt` is later than `createdAt`, validates the UUID-form idempotency-key shape and canonical unsigned payload, and exercises accepted and stale compare-and-swap outcomes.
+The fixture's `ref.canonical.update` advertisement has no constraints, so the focused ref-update authorization test does not claim to exercise constraint matching for this capability.
+Processing-time expiry rejection, reuse of a prior result for a duplicate idempotency key, durable replay prevention, and cryptographic production signature verification are later work and are not tested in Phase 0.
+
+The coordinator compares `expectedOldSha` with the current value atomically.
+On a match it records `newSha`; on a mismatch it returns a stale result without mutation.
+The node then fetches, rebases or merges, and submits a fresh signed request.
+There is no last-writer-wins behavior.
+
+## Implemented contract evidence and later work
+
+The Phase 0 fixtures and focused tests cover canonical identifier encoding, repository manifest schema validation, conflicting clone or replica rebinding rejection, one-repository catalog projection, slug conflicts, independent repository-role enforcement, grant and advertisement authorization, constraint matching, requesting-node-first routing, canonical unsigned signing-payload shape, accepted CAS, and stale rejection.
+The deterministic capability matrix uses the same five modes as the catalog and records that `node-execution` is local-first and not Qt-specific.
+
+Later phases must implement and test the real mobile identity store, Git engine, app-private replicas, Worker persistence and endpoints, signed heartbeats, host selection, mobile editing and Git operations, API agent tools, production signature verification and CAS storage, optional cross-node commands, and platform availability behavior.
+Until that work lands, contract examples describe required interoperability and do not advertise production readiness.
